#!/usr/bin/env node

// Derives the _TSS_ANCHORS layer: one point per traffic-lane leg, at which the
// style draws the lane's direction arrow.
//
// Usage:
//   bin/generate-tss-anchors <output.geojson> --class TSSLPT:TSSLPT.geojson \
//     [--class TSSRON:TSSRON.geojson] [--class DWRTPT:DWRTPT.geojson] \
//     [--class RCTLPT:RCTLPT.geojson]
//
// RCTLPT -- the recommended traffic lane -- joined the class set because it is
// a directed leg like any other and was being anchored by the generic
// per-(CLASS, LNAM, interval) path, which draws one arrow per CELL COPY of a
// lane: eight arrows, measured, on one continuous recommended lane through the
// Strait of Juan de Fuca. Only its features that CARRY ORIENT belong here; the
// rest have no leg to stitch and keep their plain centred mark from
// bin/generate-area-anchors. See ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES in bin/quilt-anchors.mjs,
// which is where that split is stated for both generators at once.
//
// Why this layer exists:
//
//   A traffic separation scheme's lane is one thing on the water and many
//   polygons in the data. S-57 splits a single lane leg at every cell boundary
//   it crosses, and the parts are separate features with DIFFERENT LNAMs, so
//   nothing in the tiles says they are the same leg. Drawing the arrow from
//   TSSLPT directly therefore draws one arrow per part.
//
//   Worse, that is not even the whole of it: tippecanoe writes a copy of a
//   feature into every tile its geometry touches, so a lane part spanning four
//   tiles arrives as four features with the same LNAM. A renderer cannot
//   collapse those at runtime -- it only ever sees the tile in front of it,
//   symbol placement is per tile, and MapLibre's `promoteId` dedup does not
//   cross tile boundaries. Deduplication has to happen where the whole
//   geometry is still in one piece, which is here.
//
//   So: group the parts back into legs, and emit ONE point per leg. A point
//   lands in exactly one tile at any zoom, which is what makes the arrow
//   appear once.
//
// Grouping is by (class, INTU) into buckets, then union-find within a bucket
// over pairs that BOTH touch and agree on ORIENT to within ORIENT_TOLERANCE.
//
// ORIENT is the leg's own traffic direction and is what distinguishes the two
// lanes of a separation scheme where they run side by side; contiguity is what
// re-joins the parts of one lane without joining the two lanes to each other.
// Class is part of the bucket key because it is carried on the output and a
// TSSLPT and a DWRTPT that happen to abut are not one leg. INTU is part of it
// for the same reason it is in bin/generate-labels: the quilt carries several
// charts at once, and two bands' copies of the same lane are not one leg --
// left ungrouped and unlabelled they draw two arrows on top of each other at
// the zoom where both bands are loaded.
//
// ORIENT is compared, NOT bucketed. Bucketing on the rounded integer split a
// real Santa Barbara lane whose four parts carry 285/285/286/285 into three
// uneven groups -- 286 is its own bucket, and the two 285 runs on either side
// of it are no longer contiguous with each other -- so one lane drew three
// arrows at uneven spacing. A tolerance comparison inside one bucket joins all
// four, and the group's own ORIENT is the area-weighted circular mean of its
// parts rather than whichever part happened to be first.
//
// Each output feature carries:
//
//   CLASS    the S-57 class the group came from (TSSLPT, TSSRON, DWRTPT,
//            RCTLPT)
//   INTU     the chart band the group came from, absent if the source had none
//   ORIENT   the group's traffic direction, degrees true, integer
//   AREA     the group's summed area in square degrees, longitude scaled by
//            cos(latitude). Kept on the output because the frontend's
//            `tss-arrows` layer filters small groups by it -- the sliver rule
//            below only removes what is too small to be a leg at all, and how
//            large a leg has to be to be worth an arrow ON SCREEN is a
//            per-zoom presentation question that belongs to the style.
//   STATION  the anchor's 0-based index along its leg, counting up the leg's
//            ORIENT direction
//   STATIONS how many stations the leg was divided into
//
// STATION/STATIONS exist so the style can thin a long leg's run of arrows by
// zoom and have every tile make the same decision: a per-tile view of the
// layer cannot know how many arrows a leg has, and MapLibre's own symbol
// collision is per tile too. STATION is stable across tiles and zooms, so
// `STATION % n == 0` thins evenly WITHIN ONE CELL'S LEG and identically in
// every tile that leg's anchors land in.
//
// Two things it deliberately does not do:
//
//   * It does not thin evenly across a cell boundary. The script runs per
//     cell (see "What this does NOT fix" below), so a lane crossing one is two
//     legs, each numbered from 0 along its own extent. `STATION % n` keeps a
//     different subset of each, and the kept arrows are evenly spaced only
//     within a cell's share of the lane.
//   * It does not disambiguate anchors for a consumer keying on
//     (ORIENT, AREA). Those two are per-GROUP and identical on every anchor of
//     a leg, and the per-cell duplication above means two groups can carry the
//     same ORIENT and near-identical AREA. STATION separates the anchors
//     within one group, not one group's anchors from another's; a consumer
//     needing a stable per-anchor identity has to key on the geometry.
//
// THE ZOOM PARTITION
//
// A partitioned cell arrives as zoom-range COPIES of each lane polygon -- one
// per interval of the copy ladder, carrying _QZMIN/_QZMAX (and _QFALL on a
// fallback continuation) -- which bin/stamp-quilt-zooms turns into per-feature
// tippecanoe zoom bounds after this runs. The interval is part of the bucket
// key for the same reason INTU is, and more strictly: the copies of one leg
// OVERLAP, so ungrouped they touch, union-find welds them into a single group
// with the summed area of all its intervals, and the one anchor that comes out
// carries no interval and draws its arrow at every zoom. The group's interval
// is carried onto its anchors. An input with none of those properties is one
// bucket per (class, INTU) and behaves exactly as it did before them.
//
// A long leg gets more than one anchor. A single arrow in the middle of a lane
// that runs the length of a strait tells the mariner nothing at either end, so
// anchors repeat every ANCHOR_SPACING_NM along the leg's own axis -- which for
// a traffic lane IS its ORIENT direction, no principal-axis fit needed.
//
// What this does NOT fix: the script runs per cell, so a leg that crosses a
// CELL boundary still gets one anchor from each cell. Those are removed by the
// same quilt clip that removes everything else the neighbouring chart covers,
// or they survive as two arrows on one long lane, which is the behaviour the
// spacing rule produces anyway.

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from "fs";

import { ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES, hasOrient } from "./quilt-anchors.mjs";

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
const inputs = [];
const positional = [];

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === "--class" && args[i + 1]) {
    const value = args[++i];
    const separator = value.indexOf(":");
    inputs.push({
      className: value.slice(0, separator),
      path: value.slice(separator + 1),
    });
  } else positional.push(args[i]);
}

const outputPath = positional[0];
if (!outputPath || inputs.length === 0) {
  console.error(
    "Usage: generate-tss-anchors <output.geojson> --class <CLASS>:<file.geojson> [--class ...]",
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

/**
 * One anchor per this many nautical miles of leg length, minimum one.
 *
 * 8 nm is roughly one arrow per screen-height at the zoom a traffic scheme
 * becomes legible (z11-z12), which is the spacing a paper chart uses. The
 * count is rounded rather than floored so the actual spacing lands as close to
 * this as the leg allows: a 20 nm leg gets 3 anchors 6.7 nm apart, not 2 at
 * 10 nm.
 */
const ANCHOR_SPACING_NM = 8;

/** Degrees of latitude per nautical mile. */
const DEGREES_PER_NM = 1 / 60;

/**
 * How close two polygons have to come to count as parts of one leg.
 *
 * The parts of a split lane are built from the same S-57 edge primitives and
 * come out of ogr2ogr with coincident coordinates, but the quilt clip runs
 * before this and re-derives the geometry arithmetically, so an exact-vertex
 * test is not safe. 1e-6 degrees is ~0.1 m -- far below the width of any lane
 * and far above the clip's rounding.
 */
const TOUCH_TOLERANCE = 1e-6;

/**
 * How far two touching parts' ORIENT may differ and still be one leg, degrees.
 *
 * ORIENT is a REAL in S-57 and encoders quantise it per part: the parts of one
 * Santa Barbara lane carry 285, 285, 286, 285. Two degrees is far below the
 * difference between any two lanes that share an edge -- the two lanes of a
 * separation scheme are 180 apart, and a lane meeting a roundabout or a
 * precautionary area differs by tens of degrees -- and far above encoder
 * quantisation.
 */
const ORIENT_TOLERANCE = 2;

/**
 * Groups shorter than this on their major axis are dropped, nautical miles.
 *
 * The quilt clip cuts lane polygons, and a cut that grazes a lane's corner
 * leaves a sliver of it behind. A sliver is still a group, and a group gets a
 * full-size arrow -- an arrow the size of the one on the 30 nm lane next to it,
 * marking a fragment a few metres across. Half a nautical mile is well below
 * any real leg (the shortest legs in a harbour approach scheme run 1-2 nm) and
 * well above what a clip leaves behind.
 *
 * "Major axis" is the LONGER of the group's two extents in its own frame, so
 * this only removes groups that are small in every direction. A leg that is
 * genuinely short but wide keeps its arrow; whether it is worth drawing at a
 * given zoom is the style's decision, made from AREA.
 */
const MIN_LEG_LENGTH_NM = 0.5;

/**
 * How far off its station an anchor may be moved to find a span to sit in.
 *
 * Expressed as a fraction of the station spacing: half a spacing either way,
 * which is the most that can be given without two anchors trading places.
 */
const STATION_SEARCH_FRACTION = 0.5;

/** Probes per side when a station lands in a gap. */
const STATION_SEARCH_STEPS = 8;

/** The smaller of the two angles between two bearings, degrees. */
function angularDistance(a, b) {
  const difference = Math.abs((((a - b) % 360) + 360) % 360);
  return difference > 180 ? 360 - difference : difference;
}

/**
 * Whether two parts' traffic directions allow them to be one leg.
 *
 * A part with no ORIENT joins only other parts with no ORIENT: "no direction"
 * is not a direction that can be within tolerance of one.
 */
function orientCompatible(a, b) {
  if (a === undefined || b === undefined) return a === b;
  return angularDistance(a, b) <= ORIENT_TOLERANCE;
}

/**
 * The group's traffic direction: the area-weighted circular mean of its parts',
 * rounded to a whole degree.
 *
 * Circular rather than arithmetic because a leg running due north has parts at
 * 359.8 and 0.2, whose arithmetic mean is 180 -- the exact opposite of the way
 * the traffic goes. Area-weighted so a clipped stub cannot pull the arrow off
 * the direction of the part that is actually the lane.
 */
function meanOrient(group) {
  if (group[0].orient === undefined) return undefined;
  const total = group.reduce((sum, member) => sum + member.area, 0);
  let x = 0;
  let y = 0;
  for (const member of group) {
    const weight = total > 0 ? member.area : 1;
    const radians = (member.orient * Math.PI) / 180;
    x += weight * Math.cos(radians);
    y += weight * Math.sin(radians);
  }
  if (x === 0 && y === 0) return Math.round(group[0].orient) % 360;
  const degrees = (Math.atan2(y, x) * 180) / Math.PI;
  return Math.round(((degrees % 360) + 360) % 360) % 360;
}

/** The partition properties a source copy carries; see THE ZOOM PARTITION. */
const QUILT_PROPERTIES = ["_QZMIN", "_QZMAX", "_QFALL"];

/** Those of them a feature actually has, to be copied onto its outputs. */
function quiltRange(properties) {
  const range = {};
  for (const name of QUILT_PROPERTIES) {
    const value = properties?.[name];
    if (value !== null && value !== undefined) range[name] = value;
  }
  return range;
}

/** The bucket key of a range. Legacy inputs all share the empty one. */
function quiltKey(range) {
  return QUILT_PROPERTIES.map((name) => range[name] ?? "").join("\u0000");
}

function readFeatures(path) {
  let text;
  try {
    text = readFileSync(path, "utf8");
  } catch (err) {
    return [];
  }
  if (!text.trim()) return [];
  return JSON.parse(text).features ?? [];
}

/** The polygons of a geometry, each as an array of rings. */
function polygons(geometry) {
  if (!geometry) return [];
  if (geometry.type === "Polygon") return [geometry.coordinates];
  if (geometry.type === "MultiPolygon") return geometry.coordinates;
  return [];
}

function ringArea(ring) {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < ring.length; i++) {
    sum += ring[i - 1][0] * ring[i][1] - ring[i][0] * ring[i - 1][1];
  }
  return sum / 2;
}

/** Signed-area centroid of a ring. */
function ringCentroid(ring) {
  let x = 0;
  let y = 0;
  let area = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < ring.length; i++) {
    const [x0, y0] = ring[i - 1];
    const [x1, y1] = ring[i];
    const cross = x0 * y1 - x1 * y0;
    area += cross;
    x += (x0 + x1) * cross;
    y += (y0 + y1) * cross;
  }
  area /= 2;
  if (area === 0) return ring[0];
  return [x / (6 * area), y / (6 * area)];
}

/** Area in square degrees, longitude scaled by cos(latitude). */
function polygonArea(polygon) {
  const latitude = (ringCentroid(polygon[0])[1] * Math.PI) / 180;
  const scale = Math.max(Math.cos(latitude), 0.01);
  let total = 0;
  // Holes are subtracted by ring INDEX, never by winding: the source leaves
  // ring orientation arbitrary, so |area| with an explicit sign is what makes
  // this hold.
  for (const ring of polygon)
    total += Math.abs(ringArea(ring)) * scale * (ring === polygon[0] ? 1 : -1);
  return Math.abs(total);
}

function bbox(polygon) {
  let minX = Infinity;
  let minY = Infinity;
  let maxX = -Infinity;
  let maxY = -Infinity;
  for (const [x, y] of polygon[0]) {
    if (x < minX) minX = x;
    if (x > maxX) maxX = x;
    if (y < minY) minY = y;
    if (y > maxY) maxY = y;
  }
  return { minX, minY, maxX, maxY };
}

function distanceToSegment(point, a, b) {
  const dx = b[0] - a[0];
  const dy = b[1] - a[1];
  const lengthSquared = dx * dx + dy * dy;
  let t = 0;
  if (lengthSquared > 0) {
    t = ((point[0] - a[0]) * dx + (point[1] - a[1]) * dy) / lengthSquared;
    t = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, t));
  }
  const px = a[0] + t * dx - point[0];
  const py = a[1] + t * dy - point[1];
  return Math.sqrt(px * px + py * py);
}

/** Whether any vertex of `from` lies within the tolerance of a ring of `to`. */
function vertexNear(from, to) {
  for (const ring of from) {
    for (const point of ring) {
      for (const other of to) {
        for (let i = 1; i < other.length; i++) {
          if (
            distanceToSegment(point, other[i - 1], other[i]) <= TOUCH_TOLERANCE
          )
            return true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return false;
}

/**
 * Whether two polygons are parts of one leg.
 *
 * Touching, not overlapping: the split parts of a lane share an edge. The
 * bounding-box gate is what keeps this from being quadratic over every vertex
 * pair in a scheme -- lane parts are long and thin and mostly nowhere near
 * each other.
 */
function touches(a, b) {
  const [ba, bb] = [bbox(a), bbox(b)];
  if (ba.maxX + TOUCH_TOLERANCE < bb.minX) return false;
  if (bb.maxX + TOUCH_TOLERANCE < ba.minX) return false;
  if (ba.maxY + TOUCH_TOLERANCE < bb.minY) return false;
  if (bb.maxY + TOUCH_TOLERANCE < ba.minY) return false;
  return vertexNear(a, b) || vertexNear(b, a);
}

/**
 * Union-find over the members of one (class, INTU) bucket.
 *
 * Two parts join when they touch AND their ORIENTs agree. The ORIENT test is
 * first because it is arithmetic and `touches` walks vertices.
 */
function contiguousGroups(members) {
  const parent = members.map((_, index) => index);
  const find = (index) => {
    let root = index;
    while (parent[root] !== root) root = parent[root];
    while (parent[index] !== root) {
      const next = parent[index];
      parent[index] = root;
      index = next;
    }
    return root;
  };
  for (let i = 0; i < members.length; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < members.length; j++) {
      if (find(i) === find(j)) continue;
      if (!orientCompatible(members[i].orient, members[j].orient)) continue;
      if (touches(members[i].polygon, members[j].polygon)) {
        parent[find(i)] = find(j);
      }
    }
  }
  const groups = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < members.length; i++) {
    const root = find(i);
    const bucket = groups.get(root);
    if (bucket) bucket.push(members[i]);
    else groups.set(root, [members[i]]);
  }
  return [...groups.values()];
}

/**
 * A local frame in which one unit is one degree of latitude in both axes, then
 * rotated so `t` runs along the leg's ORIENT and `s` across it.
 *
 * ORIENT is degrees clockwise from true north, so the along-axis unit vector
 * is (sin, cos) once longitude has been scaled by cos(latitude).
 */
function frame(orient, latitude) {
  const scale = Math.max(Math.cos((latitude * Math.PI) / 180), 0.01);
  const radians = ((orient ?? 0) * Math.PI) / 180;
  const sin = Math.sin(radians);
  const cos = Math.cos(radians);
  return {
    forward([longitude, lat]) {
      const x = longitude * scale;
      return [x * cos - lat * sin, x * sin + lat * cos]; // [s, t]
    },
    inverse([s, t]) {
      const x = t * sin + s * cos;
      const lat = t * cos - s * sin;
      return [x / scale, lat];
    },
  };
}

/**
 * The interior spans of `polygon` on the line t = station, as [s0, s1] pairs.
 *
 * The same even-odd scanline bin/generate-labels uses for its point-on-surface,
 * rotated onto the leg's axis. Holes are handled by the pairing: crossings
 * sorted along s alternate in/out.
 */
function spansAt(polygon, station) {
  const crossings = [];
  for (const ring of polygon) {
    for (let i = 1; i < ring.length; i++) {
      const [s0, t0] = ring[i - 1];
      const [s1, t1] = ring[i];
      if (t0 > station !== t1 > station) {
        crossings.push(s0 + ((station - t0) * (s1 - s0)) / (t1 - t0));
      }
    }
  }
  crossings.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const spans = [];
  for (let i = 0; i + 1 < crossings.length; i += 2) {
    spans.push([crossings[i], crossings[i + 1]]);
  }
  return spans;
}

/**
 * The widest span the GROUP has on the line t = station, as { span, width }.
 *
 * Every member's crossings go into one list and the spans that come out are
 * UNIONED -- adjacent spans whose ends meet within TOUCH_TOLERANCE become one
 * -- before the widest is picked.
 *
 * Scanning each member separately, as this did, measures each part of a split
 * lane on its own. Where a cell boundary runs ALONG a lane the two parts are
 * each half the lane's width, so the widest span is half the lane and the
 * anchor lands on the centre line of one half rather than of the lane. The
 * parts of one group are contiguous by construction (that is what grouped
 * them), so their spans at a station are pieces of one cross-section and
 * belong back together before anything is measured off them.
 */
function widestSpanAt(projected, station) {
  const spans = [];
  for (const polygon of projected) spans.push(...spansAt(polygon, station));
  if (spans.length === 0) return undefined;
  spans.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);

  let best;
  const consider = ([s0, s1]) => {
    const width = s1 - s0;
    if (!best || width > best.width) best = { span: [s0, s1], width };
  };
  let current = [spans[0][0], spans[0][1]];
  for (let i = 1; i < spans.length; i++) {
    if (spans[i][0] <= current[1] + TOUCH_TOLERANCE) {
      current[1] = Math.max(current[1], spans[i][1]);
    } else {
      consider(current);
      current = [spans[i][0], spans[i][1]];
    }
  }
  consider(current);
  return best;
}

/**
 * Where the arrows go for one group: `count` stations evenly spread along the
 * group's extent, each placed at the middle of the widest water span the group
 * has at that station.
 *
 * The mid-span point is deliberately NOT a pole of inaccessibility. On a lane
 * 30 nm long and 1 nm wide the pole is a single point at the widest bulge; the
 * mid-span at a station is the leg's centre line, which is where an arrow
 * belongs and is equally guaranteed to be inside the polygon.
 *
 * A station can land where the group has no interior at all. Grouping joins
 * parts that come within TOUCH_TOLERANCE, so a lane with a sub-tolerance gap
 * between two of its parts is one group with a hairline of nothing across it,
 * and the extent it is stationed along spans that nothing. Emitting no point
 * there silently loses an arrow from the middle of a lane -- the failure is
 * invisible in the output, which is simply one anchor shorter. So a station
 * that finds nothing is walked outward, up to half a spacing either way, to
 * the nearest line that does have interior; only if THAT finds nothing too is
 * the station dropped, and dropped stations are counted and reported.
 */
function anchorStations(projected, extent, count) {
  const { tMin, tMax } = extent;
  if (!Number.isFinite(tMin)) return { points: [], dropped: 0 };

  const spacing = (tMax - tMin) / count;
  const reach = spacing * STATION_SEARCH_FRACTION;
  const points = [];
  let dropped = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    const station = tMin + spacing * (i + 0.5);
    let best = widestSpanAt(projected, station);
    let placedAt = station;

    // Outward from the station, so the arrow lands as close to where it
    // belongs as the geometry allows.
    for (let step = 1; !best && step <= STATION_SEARCH_STEPS; step++) {
      const offset = (reach * step) / STATION_SEARCH_STEPS;
      for (const candidate of [station - offset, station + offset]) {
        const found = widestSpanAt(projected, candidate);
        if (found && (!best || found.width > best.width)) {
          best = found;
          placedAt = candidate;
        }
      }
    }

    // `index` is the station's own place along the leg and is carried to the
    // output as STATION. A dropped station leaves a GAP in the sequence
    // rather than renumbering the ones after it, so the indices stay evenly
    // spaced along the leg and a frontend thinning on `STATION % n` thins
    // evenly whatever was dropped here.
    if (best)
      points.push({
        point: [(best.span[0] + best.span[1]) / 2, placedAt],
        index: i,
      });
    else dropped++;
  }
  return { points, dropped };
}

/** The extent of one projected polygon in the group frame. */
function polygonExtent(polygon) {
  let sMin = Infinity;
  let sMax = -Infinity;
  let tMin = Infinity;
  let tMax = -Infinity;
  for (const ring of polygon) {
    for (const [s, t] of ring) {
      if (s < sMin) sMin = s;
      if (s > sMax) sMax = s;
      if (t < tMin) tMin = t;
      if (t > tMax) tMax = t;
    }
  }
  return { sMin, sMax, tMin, tMax };
}

/** The group's extent in its own frame: `t` along the leg, `s` across it. */
function axisExtent(projected) {
  let sMin = Infinity;
  let sMax = -Infinity;
  let tMin = Infinity;
  let tMax = -Infinity;
  for (const polygon of projected) {
    const part = polygonExtent(polygon);
    if (part.sMin < sMin) sMin = part.sMin;
    if (part.sMax > sMax) sMax = part.sMax;
    if (part.tMin < tMin) tMin = part.tMin;
    if (part.tMax > tMax) tMax = part.tMax;
  }
  return { sMin, sMax, tMin, tMax };
}

/**
 * The members of a group that carry the run of arrows: the "bearers".
 *
 * A quilt clip that grazes a lane leaves a crumb attached to it. The crumb
 * touches the lane and carries the lane's ORIENT, so it is part of the group
 * -- correctly, it is part of the lane's area -- but it is not part of the
 * lane's BODY, and two things went wrong when it was treated as if it were:
 *
 *   * the group's along-axis extent stretched over the crumb's end, so
 *     stations were laid out over ground the lane does not cover, and
 *   * at such a station the crumb was the only member with any interior, so
 *     it owned the station outright and the arrow landed at the crumb's
 *     centre -- measured at 82.5% of the lane's half-width off the lane's
 *     own centre line.
 *
 * A member is excluded only when it is BOTH:
 *
 *   * narrow -- its ACROSS-axis extent is under SLIVER_WIDTH_FRACTION of the
 *     widest member's, and
 *   * short -- its ALONG-axis extent is under one ANCHOR_SPACING_NM, i.e. it
 *     is not long enough to have wanted an anchor of its own.
 *
 * Width alone is not enough, and testing it alone was wrong. A lane that
 * TAPERS -- narrows and carries on, which is what a lane does where it feeds a
 * precautionary area or a harbour approach -- is narrow by this measure for
 * its whole length, so a width-only test threw the continuation off the
 * station axis and the tail of the lane lost every one of its arrows. An 18 nm
 * continuation at 15% of the lane's width is 18 nm of traffic lane with no
 * direction arrow on it. A clip crumb fails BOTH conditions and is still
 * excluded; a tapering lane fails only the width one and keeps its arrows.
 *
 * The relative width test is why the along-axis condition is needed rather
 * than an absolute width: lane widths are not comparable between a strait and
 * a harbour approach, so "narrow" can only ever mean "narrow for this group",
 * and a group whose real shape is a taper has no wide member to be narrow
 * against. Length is the thing a crumb cannot fake.
 *
 * The widest member is always a bearer, so the set is never empty. Non-bearers
 * still count towards the group's AREA, its mean ORIENT, and -- since the span
 * union sees every member -- the cross-section an anchor is centred in; they
 * just do not stretch the axis the stations are laid out along.
 */
const SLIVER_WIDTH_FRACTION = 0.2;

/**
 * Slack on the width floor, as a fraction of it.
 *
 * The comparison runs on ROTATED and cos(latitude)-SCALED coordinates, so a
 * member built as exactly SLIVER_WIDTH_FRACTION of the widest lands a few ulps
 * either side of the floor depending on the group's bearing and latitude.
 * Without the slack an exactly-at-the-floor member is a coin toss, and the
 * documented "at least this fraction" is inclusive.
 */
const SLIVER_FLOOR_EPSILON = 1e-9;

function bearingMembers(projected) {
  const extents = projected.map(polygonExtent);
  let widest = 0;
  for (const { sMin, sMax } of extents) {
    if (Number.isFinite(sMin) && sMax - sMin > widest) widest = sMax - sMin;
  }
  const floor = widest * SLIVER_WIDTH_FRACTION * (1 - SLIVER_FLOOR_EPSILON);
  const bearers = projected.filter((_, index) => {
    const { sMin, sMax, tMin, tMax } = extents[index];
    if (!Number.isFinite(sMin) || !Number.isFinite(tMin)) return false;
    const narrow = sMax - sMin < floor;
    const short = (tMax - tMin) / DEGREES_PER_NM < ANCHOR_SPACING_NM;
    return !(narrow && short);
  });
  return bearers.length > 0 ? bearers : projected;
}

/** The along-axis extent in nautical miles: what the anchor count divides. */
function axisLengthNm({ tMin, tMax }) {
  return Number.isFinite(tMin) ? (tMax - tMin) / DEGREES_PER_NM : 0;
}

/** The longer of the two extents in nautical miles; see MIN_LEG_LENGTH_NM. */
function majorAxisNm({ sMin, sMax, tMin, tMax }) {
  if (!Number.isFinite(tMin) || !Number.isFinite(sMin)) return 0;
  return Math.max(tMax - tMin, sMax - sMin) / DEGREES_PER_NM;
}

// ---- 1. bucket every polygon by (class, ORIENT) ----------------------------

const buckets = new Map();

for (const { className, path } of inputs) {
  for (const feature of readFeatures(path)) {
    // Kept as the REAL that S-57 carries. Rounding HERE is what split a lane
    // whose parts read 285/285/286/285 into three groups; the tolerance
    // comparison in the grouping absorbs that jitter instead, and only the
    // group's mean is ever rounded.
    const orient = hasOrient(feature.properties)
      ? ((Number(feature.properties.ORIENT) % 360) + 360) % 360
      : undefined;
    // A directed-leg class with no direction is not a leg. RCTLPT without
    // ORIENT draws the plain SY(RTLDEF51) mark, which bin/generate-area-anchors
    // anchors; see ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES for the split.
    if (orient === undefined && ORIENT_LEG_CLASSES.has(className)) continue;
    const intu = feature.properties?.INTU ?? null;
    const range = quiltRange(feature.properties);
    const id = `${className}\u0000${intu}\u0000${quiltKey(range)}`;
    for (const polygon of polygons(feature.geometry)) {
      if (!polygon[0] || polygon[0].length < 4) continue;
      const bucket = buckets.get(id);
      const member = {
        className,
        intu,
        range,
        orient,
        polygon,
        // Kept on the member: it weights the group's mean ORIENT as well as
        // summing into the group's AREA.
        area: polygonArea(polygon),
      };
      if (bucket) bucket.push(member);
      else buckets.set(id, [member]);
    }
  }
}

const features = [];
let slivers = 0;
let droppedStations = 0;

for (const members of buckets.values()) {
  for (const group of contiguousGroups(members)) {
    const { className, intu, range } = group[0];
    // Parts of one leg only touch, so the areas do not overlap and their sum
    // is the union's area.
    const area = group.reduce((total, member) => total + member.area, 0);
    const orient = meanOrient(group);
    const latitude =
      group.reduce(
        (total, member) => total + ringCentroid(member.polygon[0])[1],
        0,
      ) / group.length;

    const { forward, inverse } = frame(orient, latitude);
    const projected = group.map((member) =>
      member.polygon.map((ring) => ring.map(forward)),
    );

    // Stations are laid out over the members that are actually the lane's
    // body, so a crumb hanging off it does not extend the run of arrows.
    //
    // With no ORIENT there is no lane axis: `frame` falls back to north-up, so
    // "across-axis extent" is just the member's longitude span and means
    // nothing about the group's shape. On a roundabout or a precautionary area
    // -- exactly the classes that carry no ORIENT -- that measure can exclude
    // the member the group mostly IS: an east-west connector at the end of a
    // north-south lane is the wider of the two in longitude, so the lane was
    // dropped and the group's one anchor landed 11 nm away, inside the
    // connector. Skip the test for the whole group instead, which is what this
    // did before the sliver rule existed.
    const bearers =
      orient === undefined ? projected : bearingMembers(projected);
    const extent = axisExtent(bearers);
    if (majorAxisNm(extent) < MIN_LEG_LENGTH_NM) {
      slivers++;
      continue;
    }

    // A group with no ORIENT (a roundabout, say) has no axis to repeat along.
    const count =
      orient === undefined
        ? 1
        : Math.max(1, Math.round(axisLengthNm(extent) / ANCHOR_SPACING_NM));

    // Stations come from the bearers' extent but are MEASURED against every
    // member. The span union in `widestSpanAt` exists to put a lengthwise-split
    // lane's cross-section back together before the anchor is centred in it,
    // and handing it bearers only defeated that for exactly the split it was
    // written for: where a cell boundary runs along a lane and leaves an 85/15
    // pair of strips, the 15% strip is a non-bearer, the union sees one strip,
    // and the anchor centres on the 85% strip's own middle rather than the
    // lane's. A non-bearer is still lane; it just does not get a say in where
    // the stations are.
    const { points, dropped } = anchorStations(projected, extent, count);
    droppedStations += dropped;

    for (const { point, index } of points) {
      features.push({
        type: "Feature",
        // NOT a property: tippecanoe reads this member off the Feature itself.
        // It drops a fraction of POINT features at every zoom below the
        // maxzoom (-r/--drop-rate, default 2.5), and a dropped anchor is an
        // arrow missing from a traffic lane. --drop-rate is a whole-run knob,
        // so turning it off would also stop the SOUNDG thinning that keeps
        // small-scale tiles readable; a per-feature `minzoom` is the only
        // exemption tippecanoe scopes, and it is documented as preserving the
        // feature "even if dot-dropping with -r would otherwise have dropped
        // it". Zero, so it applies from whatever the tileset's own minimum
        // zoom turns out to be. See bin/s57-to-tiles step 4.
        tippecanoe: { minzoom: 0 },
        geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: inverse(point) },
        properties: {
          CLASS: className,
          ...(intu === null ? {} : { INTU: intu }),
          ...(orient === undefined ? {} : { ORIENT: orient }),
          AREA: area,
          // The anchor's place in its leg's run of anchors, and how long that
          // run is. Both are here so the frontend can thin a long leg EVENLY
          // by zoom -- keep every anchor at z12, every other one at z11, and
          // so on -- which it cannot do from a per-tile view of the layer,
          // because the thinning has to be the same decision in every tile
          // and STATION is the only thing that is the same in every tile.
          STATION: index,
          STATIONS: count,
          // The interval the group's members serve, converted to tippecanoe
          // bounds later by bin/stamp-quilt-zooms.
          ...range,
        },
      });
    }
  }
}

writeFileSync(
  outputPath,
  JSON.stringify({ type: "FeatureCollection", features }),
);
console.error(
  `Generated ${features.length} TSS anchors` +
    (slivers > 0 ? `, ${slivers} sliver group(s) dropped` : ""),
);
// Reported rather than swallowed: a dropped station is an arrow missing from
// the middle of a lane, and nothing about the output says one is missing.
if (droppedStations > 0) {
  console.error(
    `  WARNING: ${droppedStations} anchor station(s) found no interior span ` +
      `within half a station spacing and were dropped`,
  );
}
